---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';

const title = 'EB-5 for EB-3 Backlog Applicants | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'EB-5 for EB-3 backlog applicants covers why skilled, professional, and other workers with an EB-3 track sometimes evaluate EB-5 as a parallel or alternative path — with attorney review first.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb3-to-eb5`;
const h1 = 'EB-5 for EB-3 Backlog Applicants';
const subhead =
  'How EB-5 can enter the conversation when an EB-3 employment-based case faces long visa-number waits.';

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'EB-5 Immigrant Investor Program — Complete Guide',
    url: '/research/complete-2027-eb5-guide',
    description: 'Program structure, reserved categories, and filing overview.',
  },
  {
    title: 'H-1B to Green Card via EB-5',
    url: '/pathways/h1b-to-green-card',
    description: 'Employer-independence and concurrent filing framing for H-1B households.',
  },
];
---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone About EB-5 Alongside an EB-3 Case"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for EB-3 Backlog"
  keywords={[
    'EB-5 for EB-3 backlog',
    'EB-3 to EB-5',
    'skilled worker green card backlog',
    'EB-3 parallel EB-5',
  ]}
>
  <h2>What sits behind an EB-3 petition</h2>
  <p>
    EB-3 covers a broader employment-based immigrant group than EB-2: skilled workers, professionals, and other
    workers, typically through employer sponsorship. That breadth means many households reach I-140 approval through
    different job classifications and industries, yet still share the same structural constraint afterward — immigrant
    visa numbers allocated by country of birth. When demand exceeds supply for that chargeability, the green card can
    remain unavailable long after the labor and petition stages are done. Check live category movement on the
    <a
      href="https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/legal/visa-law0/visa-bulletin.html"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">Visa Bulletin</a
    > rather than relying on remembered wait times.
  </p>

  <h2>Why EB-5 shows up for EB-3 households</h2>
  <p>
    EB-5 does not replace the need for a qualifying investment or USCIS petition standards, but it does change the
    sponsorship model: the investor is the petitioner through capital and job creation, not through a job offer alone.
    Some EB-3 applicants explore that structure when the employment-based queue feels unpredictable, when a layoff or
    job change could disrupt sponsorship, or when family members need a path that is not locked to one employer's
    timeline. Reserved EB-5 categories and concurrent filing (for those already in the U.S.) are part of that evaluation
    — not promises of a shorter wait. Start with the
    <a href="/research/complete-2027-eb5-guide">complete EB-5 guide</a>; for H-1B-adjacent concurrent filing context, see
    <a href="/pathways/h1b-to-green-card">H-1B to green card via EB-5</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Running EB-5 next to an EB-3 case</h2>
  <p>
    An EB-5 filing does not automatically force you to withdraw an EB-3 petition. Whether dual tracks are workable —
    including how adjustment of status, immigrant visa processing, and derivative family members interact across
    categories — depends on the specific record. Some families keep the employment-based case alive while preparing
    source-of-funds documentation for EB-5; others consolidate once an investment path is chosen. Get case-specific
    advice from an immigration attorney before pursuing both or choosing between them.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
